_id-1 is free OR adjacent seat_id+1 is free). So it outputs a free seat if at least one of its immediate neighbors is free. Q2 outputs a free seat if it belongs to a group of ≥2 consecutive free seats. So the difference is that Q1 can output a free seat that is at the edge of a consecutive block, but Q2 outputs all seats in blocks of size ≥2. For example, seats 1,2,3 free: both output {1,2,3}. Seats 1,2 free, seat 3 occupied: both output {1,2}. Seats 1 free, seat 2 free, seat 3 occupied: both output {1,2}. No difference. Actually, consider seats 1 free, seat 2 free, seat 3 free, seat 4 occupied. Both output {1,2,3}. The difference arises when there is a free seat that has an adjacent free seat, but that adjacent free seat is not consecutive in the sequence of free seats? That's impossible because if seat A and seat B are adjacent (|seat_id diff| = 1) and both free, they are consecutive free seats. So Q1 and Q2 should always agree for adjacent free seats. But Q2 uses SEAT_ID - ROW_NUMBER() to group consecutive free seats. For free seats ordered by SEAT_ID, if they are consecutive in SEAT_ID, then SEAT_ID - ROW_NUMBER() is constant. For example, free seats at IDs 1,2,3: ROW_NUMBER 1,2,3; differences 0,0,0; constant group. So Q2 outputs all. If free seats are at IDs 1,3: ROW_NUMBER 1,2; differences 0,1; not constant, so no group of size ≥2. So Q2 only outputs seats that are part of a consecutive sequence of free seats by SEAT_ID. Q1 outputs a free seat if it has any immediate neighbor that is free. So if there is a free seat with an immediate neighbor that is free, but that neighbor is not part of a consecutive sequence? For example, seats 2 and 4 free, seat 3 occupied. Then seat 2 has neighbor 1 and 3; neither free, so seat 2 not output; seat 4 has neighbor 3 and 5; neither free, so seat 4 not output. So Q1 outputs nothing. Q2: free seats 2,4; ROW_NUMBER 1,2; differences 1,2; no group. So both output nothing. To get a difference, we need a case where Q1 outputs a seat