if S is part of a pair of consecutive free seats. That is exactly the same as Q1, because Q1 outputs S if S has an adjacent free seat, which means there exists T with |seat_id diff| = 1 and T.free=1. So Q1 and Q2 are equivalent. But the problem asks for a counterexample, so they must not be equivalent. Let's look at Q2 again: WITH VALID_SEAT AS (SELECT SEAT_ID, ROW_NUMBER() OVER(ORDER BY SEAT_ID) AS NUM FROM CINEMA WHERE FREE=1) SELECT SEAT_ID FROM (SELECT *, COUNT(1) OVER(PARTITION BY SEAT_ID - NUM)AS C FROM VALID_SEAT)B WHERE C >=2. This selects seats from VALID_SEAT where the count of seats in the same partition (SEAT_ID - NUM) is at least 2. SEAT_ID - NUM is constant for consecutive free seats. So it outputs seats that are in groups of at least 2 consecutive free seats. Q1 outputs seats that are free and have at least one adjacent free seat. These are the same condition. Proof: If seat S is in a group of at least 2 consecutive free seats, then it has an adjacent free seat (the next or previous in the group). Conversely, if seat S has an adjacent free seat T, then S and T form a group of 2 consecutive free seats, so S is in a group of size ≥2. Therefore, the queries are equivalent. But perhaps there is an edge case with seat IDs that are not sequential? The schema allows any INT. Consider seats with IDs 1 and 100, both free, and no other

