explanation:
{
    "explanation": "Q1 selects products whose sales all occurred within Q1 2019 (min and max sale dates both within that period). Q2 selects products that have at least one sale in Q1 2019 and no sales outside that period. The key difference is that Q1 requires ALL sales of a product to be within the period, while Q2 only requires the product to have SOME sales within the period and NO sales outside it. This means a product with multiple sales, some within and some outside Q1 2019, will be excluded by Q1 but included by Q2 if it has at least one sale in Q1 2019 and no sales outside Q1 2019.",
    "counterexample_dsp": "A product with multiple sales, where at least one sale is within Q1 2019 and all sales are within Q1 2019, but with sales both before and after the period. Q1 excludes it because the min sale date is before 2019-01-01 or max sale date is after 2019-03-31. Q2 includes it because it has at least one sale in Q1 2019 and no sales outside Q1 2019 (but this is impossible given the min/max condition). Actually, the correct difference is: a product with sales only within Q1 2019 will be included by both. A product with sales both within and outside Q1 2019 will be excluded by both. The real difference is when a product has no sales at all. Q1 excludes it (no matching PRODUCT_ID in the subquery). Q2 includes it because the LEFT JOIN from SALES will produce NULL for PRODUCTS_IN_FIRST and PRODUCTS_IN_REST, and the WHERE clause requires PRODUCTS_IN_FIRST.IS_IN_FIRST IS TRUE, which fails for NULL, so it's excluded too. Wait, let's re-examine Q2: it starts with SALES LEFT JOIN PRODUCT, so if a product has no sales, it won't appear in SALES, so it won't be in the result. So both exclude products with no sales. The actual difference is when a product has sales only outside Q1 2019. Q1 excludes it (min and max dates outside the period). Q2: PRODUCTS_IN_FIRST will be NULL (no sales in period), PRODUCTS_IN_REST will be TRUE (sales outside period), WHERE requires PRODUCTS_IN_FIRST.IS_IN_FIRST IS TRUE, which fails, so excluded. So both exclude. Hmm. Let's trace Q2 more carefully: PRODUCTS_IN_FIRST finds products with sales in Q1 2019. PRODUCTS_IN_REST finds products with sales outside Q1 2019. The main query joins SALES with these and filters for PRODUCTS_IN_FIRST.IS_IN_FIRST IS TRUE AND PRODUCTS_IN_REST.IS_IN_REST IS NOT TRUE. This means: product must have at least one sale in Q1 2019, and must not have any sales outside Q1 2019. But note: if a product has sales only in Q1 2019, it will have a row in PRODUCTS_IN_FIRST (TRUE) and no row in PRODUCTS_IN_REST (so IS_IN_REST IS NULL, and IS NOT TRUE is true). So included. If a product has sales both inside and outside Q1 2019, it will have rows in both, so IS_IN_REST IS TRUE, and IS NOT TRUE fails, excluded. If a product has sales only outside Q1 2019, it will have no row in PRODUCTS_IN_FIRST (so IS_IN_FIRST IS NULL, and IS TRUE fails), excluded. So Q2 selects products that have at least one sale in Q1 2019 and no sales outside Q1 2019. Q1 selects products where all sales are within Q1 2019 (min date >= 2019-01-01 and max date <= 2019-03-31). These are equivalent? Actually, if a product has all sales within Q1 2019, then it has at least one sale (unless no sales, but then not in result) and no sales outside. So they seem equivalent. But wait: Q1 uses MIN and MAX. If a product has sales only in Q1 2019, min and max will be within, so included. If it has sales both inside and outside, min or max will be outside, excluded. So they are equivalent. But there's a subtlety: Q2 uses DISTINCT in the CTEs and main query, which removes duplicates. Q1 uses GROUP BY, which also effectively removes duplicates per product in the subquery. So still equivalent. The difference might be in handling of products with no sales? Both exclude. Actually, the difference is in the join: Q1 uses INNER JOIN between PRODUCT and the subquery, so only products with sales meeting the condition. Q2 starts from SALES, so only products with at least one sale in SALES. So both require at least one sale. So they are equivalent. But wait, Q2 has LEFT JOIN from SALES to PRODUCT, so if a product has sales but no matching row in PRODUCT (violating foreign key? but foreign key ensures match), still product info might be NULL. But PRODUCT_ID is from SALES, so it's fine. So they are equivalent. But the problem asks for a counterexample, so there must be a difference. Let's examine NULL handling: Q2 has LEFT JOINs to PRODUCTS_IN_FIRST and PRODUCTS_IN_REST. For a product with sales only in Q1 2019, PRODUCTS_IN_FIRST will