    "explanation": "Q1 selects products where all sales for that product occurred within Jan-Mar 2019 (using MIN and MAX on sale dates). Q2 selects products where there exists at least one sale within Jan-Mar 2019 AND no sales outside that period. The key difference is that Q1 requires at least one sale (since GROUP BY on PRODUCT_ID in SALES requires at least one row), while Q2's EXISTS subquery also requires at least one sale within the period. However, Q2 additionally filters out products with any sales outside the period, while Q1's HAVING condition only checks the MIN and MAX dates of existing sales. If a product has no sales at all, Q1 will exclude it (no matching PRODUCT_ID in SALES), but Q2 will also exclude it (EXISTS subquery fails). The real divergence occurs when a product has sales both inside and outside the period: Q1 includes it if MIN and MAX fall within the period (meaning all sales are inside), while Q2 excludes it if any sale is outside (due to NOT EXISTS).",
    "counterexample_dsp": "A product with sales both inside and outside Jan-Mar 2019. For example, product X has one sale on '2019-02-01' (inside period) and another on '2019-04-01' (outside period). Q1 excludes X because MAX(SALE_DATE) = '2019-04-01' > '2019-03-31', so the HAVING condition fails. Q2 excludes X because the NOT EXISTS subquery finds the sale on '2019-04-01' (outside period), making the overall WHERE condition false. Wait, this would make both queries exclude X, not a difference. Actually, the difference arises when a product has sales only inside the period but with multiple sales: both queries include it. The divergence is when a product has no sales at all: Q1 excludes it (no join match), Q2 excludes it (EXISTS fails). That's not a difference either. Let's reconsider: Q1's subquery groups by PRODUCT_ID and filters groups where MIN and MAX dates are within the period. If a product has sales spanning outside the period, it's excluded. Q2's EXISTS finds at least one sale inside period, and NOT EXISTS ensures no sales outside. If a product has sales both inside and outside, Q2 excludes it, Q1 excludes it. If a product has sales only inside, both include. If no sales, both exclude. Where's the difference? The subtlety: Q1's HAVING checks MIN >= '2019-01-01' AND MAX <= '2019-03-31'. If a product has sales, but the earliest sale is before '2019-01-01' or the latest after '2019-03-31', it's excluded. Q2's NOT EXISTS checks for any sale outside the period. These are logically equivalent for products with sales. But consider a product with sales only outside the period, e.g., all sales in 2018. Q1: MIN and MAX are outside, so HAVING fails, excluded. Q2: EXISTS subquery looks for a sale inside period—none, so EXISTS fails, excluded. Still same. The difference is in handling of products with no sales: both exclude, so no difference. Wait, maybe I missed: Q1's subquery selects PRODUCT_ID from SALES group by PRODUCT_ID having... If a product has no sales, it won